course: dictionary
lesson: 2102
title: One word, three meanings: contract
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «She signed a contract for her new phone.» A contract is a written promise that the law can make people keep. Here it is a thing you sign, so it is a noun.
explain: Now read this: «In the cold, the metal rod began to contract.» Metal cannot sign anything, so contract must be an action. It means the rod gets smaller and shorter. Ask yourself: is this word a thing or an action? That question helps you choose the right meaning.
explain: The word can also mean to catch an illness: «He contracted a cold after the football match.» Try the meaning in the sentence. "He caught a cold" fits, so this is the right meaning. If your meaning makes the sentence sound silly, try another one.
fact: A contract is a written or spoken agreement that the law can make people keep.
fact: To contract means to get smaller or shorter, like a muscle or metal when it cools.
fact: To contract an illness means to catch it.
fact: «Most materials expand when they are heated and contract when they are cooled; metal expands more than glass.»
fact: «A phone contract is an agreement to pay for a set time: £25 a month for 24 months is 24 × £25 = £600 in total.»
exercise: «She signed a contract for her new phone.» What is a contract here? | *A written agreement that the law can make people keep | A kind of cold drink | A muscle in your leg
exercise: «In the cold, the metal rod began to contract.» What does contract mean here? | To sign a promise | To get bigger and wider | To catch a cold | *To get smaller and shorter
exercise: «He contracted a cold after the football match.» What does contracted mean here? | To sign a deal | To cool down a room | *To catch an illness | To grow taller
test: «On a zero-hours contract the hours and the pay can change every week.» Why can the pay change each week? | *Because the employer promises no set number of hours | Because the worker chooses a new contract every week | Because it is a holiday | Because the pay is always exactly the same
test: «Metal expands when it is heated and contracts when it is cooled.» Which pair of meanings is correct? | Expand means get smaller, contract means get bigger | Both mean sign a promise | *Expand means get bigger, contract means get smaller | Both mean catch an illness
exam: «The small print in a contract gives the detail, and once you sign you usually have to keep to it.» What does this sentence tell you about signing a contract? | *You usually have to keep to what you agreed | You can always change your mind the next day | Contracts are only for rich people | The small print is never important
